It's 100% based on our capacity

1604
01:08:46.940 --> 01:08:48.410
to see it as it's happening.

1605
01:08:48.410 --> 01:08:50.730
I mean this right here, there's nothing.

1606
01:08:50.730 --> 01:08:52.460
I remember thinking that this morning,

1607
01:08:52.460 --> 01:08:55.420
it was cold this morning, it was windy this morning.

1608
01:08:55.420 --> 01:08:57.090
There's construction going on.

1609
01:08:57.090 --> 01:09:00.919
It's loud, there's a bunch of cars driving away too fast,

1610
01:09:00.919 --> 01:09:03.942
but it was also, the sun was out.

1611
01:09:03.942 --> 01:09:05.833
I could hear the ocean.

1612
01:09:06.670 --> 01:09:11.670
I can breathe without carrying oxygen with me.

1613
01:09:11.930 --> 01:09:13.383
I can eat through my mouth.

1614
01:09:13.383 --> 01:09:17.633
I don't have a tube anymore in my stomach.

1615
01:09:19.700 --> 01:09:21.930
I can take a shower by myself.

1616
01:09:21.930 --> 01:09:24.190
I don't have to bring oxygen into there.

1617
01:09:24.190 --> 01:09:26.160
My heart rate doesn't go to 190

1618
01:09:26.160 --> 01:09:28.160
just because the hot water hits my body.

1619
01:09:29.820 --> 01:09:31.540
I can use the toilet by myself.

1620
01:09:31.540 --> 01:09:32.780
I don't have to wear a diaper.

1621
01:09:32.780 --> 01:09:34.230
I don't have bedsores right now.

1622
01:09:34.230 --> 01:09:38.020
I don't have to piss out of a catheter.

1623
01:09:38.020 --> 01:09:39.313
All of those things.

1624
01:09:41.290 --> 01:09:44.530
The absence of all of those things is remarkable.

1625
01:09:44.530 --> 01:09:46.791
I mean, it's incredible, it's a miracle.

1626
01:09:46.791 --> 01:09:51.183
And the ability to see that right now,

1627
01:09:52.730 --> 01:09:54.880
this situation, that there's nothing

1628
01:09:57.200 --> 01:09:58.400
that could make it any better.

1629
01:09:58.400 --> 01:10:00.600
I mean, I have contact with my family.

1630
01:10:00.600 --> 01:10:01.780
I saw them just a couple of days ago,

1631
01:10:01.780 --> 01:10:03.003
I'll see them tomorrow.

1632
01:10:04.300 --> 01:10:07.420
I have my girls, I have a house, I have a car,

1633
01:10:07.420 --> 01:10:08.600
we have climate control.

1634
01:10:08.600 --> 01:10:10.687
We have all of this stuff,

1635
01:10:10.687 --> 01:10:13.060
there's nothing that can make it any better, nothing.

1636
01:10:13.060 --> 01:10:16.808
I mean, really, I sincerely believe that here's nothing

1637
01:10:16.808 --> 01:10:20.360
that could improve this situation.

1638
01:10:20.360 --> 01:10:21.370
But here's the thing,

1639
01:10:21.370 --> 01:10:24.640
I feel like you're somebody who already understood that,

1640
01:10:24.640 --> 01:10:26.910
like you've conducted your life in accordance

1641
01:10:26.910 --> 01:10:29.340
with those values all along.

1642
01:10:29.340 --> 01:10:33.370
I suspect maybe you're in a graduate degree program

1643
01:10:33.370 --> 01:10:38.145
with that, but it's not like you were living your life

1644
01:10:38.145 --> 01:10:42.730
antithetically to those principles prior to your illness.

1645
01:10:42.730 --> 01:10:46.140
Like you seem like a guy who understood that before.

1646
01:10:46.140 --> 01:10:48.166
Maybe, I mean maybe in theory,

1647
01:10:48.166 --> 01:10:52.937
but I don't know, I'm cynical.

1648
01:10:52.937 --> 01:10:57.753
I'm grumpy, a lot of what comes out,

1649
01:11:04.090 --> 01:11:05.590
whether I say it or whether I write it

1650
01:11:05.590 --> 01:11:10.590
or however it's expressed is the conversation

1651
01:11:11.680 --> 01:11:15.280
that I'm having with myself, trying to help myself realign,

1652
01:11:15.280 --> 01:11:17.853
trying to help myself focus on what's good,

1653
01:11:21.820 --> 01:11:23.030
facing a challenge,

1654
01:11:23.030 --> 01:11:26.060
being honest about what that challenge consists of,

1655
01:11:26.060 --> 01:11:29.450
figuring out what I can control and focusing on that

1656
01:11:29.450 --> 01:11:34.143
and then spinning a positive narrative around it.

1657
01:11:36.690 --> 01:11:37.523
Excuse me.

1658
01:11:41.160 --> 01:11:46.160
But gosh, it's always been the conversation

1659
01:11:47.140 --> 01:11:51.283
that's going on in my head, that inner dialogue I guess,

1660
01:11:53.350 --> 01:11:58.350
to try to face whatever it is that I'm going through,

1661
01:12:02.570 --> 01:12:04.240
I guess, in an optimistic sense.

1662
01:12:04.240 --> 01:12:09.240
But that doesn't mean it's ever been easy,

1663
01:12:19.980 --> 01:12:21.800
gosh, I don't know.

1664
01:12:22.942 --> 01:12:24.142
In this whole situation,

1665
01:12:26.180 --> 01:12:28.160
the things that have been the most challenging

1666
01:12:28.160 --> 01:12:32.420
are things that you wouldn't necessarily think about.

1667
01:12:32.420 --> 01:12:34.010
I wouldn't have thought about.

1668
01:12:34.010 --> 01:12:34.843
Like what?

1669
01:12:41.600 --> 01:12:46.587
Waking up addicted to pain, gosh, that's been.

1670
01:12:49.410 --> 01:12:51.476
What did they have you on?

1671
01:12:51.476 --> 01:12:54.520
It's like a battery of opiods, morphine.

1672
01:12:54.520 --> 01:12:59.437
Yeah, fentanyl morphine and that stuff is lovely.

1673
01:13:05.120 --> 01:13:09.080
I don't think I've ever grieved the loss of something

1674
01:13:15.220 --> 01:13:17.230
as much as that.

1675
01:13:17.230 --> 01:13:22.230
Yeah, it becomes everything, that's how you get through,

1676
01:13:25.140 --> 01:13:30.140
that's how you get through those insurances.

1677
01:13:32.565 --> 01:13:34.290
It's like how do people endure that?

1678
01:13:34.290 --> 01:13:35.610
It's like, well you endure it

1679
01:13:35.610 --> 01:13:38.443
because you have a drink of morphine, right?

1680
01:13:39.660 --> 01:13:44.587
That stuffs 50 times more potent than heroin,

1681
01:13:45.676 --> 01:13:50.676
and coming off of that, that in combination with,

1682
01:13:52.270 --> 01:13:53.680
you're on everything and you're on it

1683
01:13:53.680 --> 01:13:55.520
because you need to be on it.

1684
01:13:55.520 --> 01:13:59.580
I mean, I don't know how you could endure

1685
01:13:59.580 --> 01:14:02.900
what you go through, if you weren't heavily medicated.

1686
01:14:02.900 --> 01:14:05.891
But then when the time comes to, a lot of the times,

1687
01:14:05.891 --> 01:14:07.870
it seems as though you're medicated

1688
01:14:07.870 --> 01:14:09.680
because they don't actually think

1689
01:14:09.680 --> 01:14:10.513
you're going to make it through.

1690
01:14:10.513 --> 01:14:12.690
So it's like, okay, make this as comfortable as possible.

1691
01:14:12.690 --> 01:14:13.950
This is pain management, yeah.

1692
01:14:13.950 --> 01:14:16.113
Yeah as you transition out and then,

1693
01:14:18.890 --> 01:14:20.040
well then you don't die.

1694
01:14:20.040 --> 01:14:21.720
And then it's like, oh, damn okay.

1695
01:14:21.720 --> 01:14:25.413
We've got to figure out how to get your body

1696
01:14:25.413 --> 01:14:28.474
to where it can function without all of this.

1697
01:14:28.474 --> 01:14:31.492
And that was hard.

1698
01:14:31.492 --> 01:14:34.943
That's still hard.

1699
01:14:37.945 --> 01:14:42.330
I mean to say that I don't think the pull ever goes away

1700
01:14:43.822 --> 01:14:47.880
and you just have to jump it, from that to something else.

1701
01:14:47.880 --> 01:14:51.740
It'll slowly, slowly fade into the background, but yeah,

1702
01:14:51.740 --> 01:14:53.700
it never quite goes away.

1703
01:14:53.700 --> 01:14:56.862
It's always lurking in the background, man.

1704
01:14:56.862 --> 01:15:01.343
Well, it's been something that it's not new to me.

1705
01:15:09.230 --> 01:15:11.130
I think addiction is so ubiquitous

1706
01:15:11.130 --> 01:15:14.160
that I have a hard time even referring

1707
01:15:14.160 --> 01:15:17.460
to people's as addicts.

1708
01:15:17.460 --> 01:15:19.100
'Cause I think that people are humans

1709
01:15:19.100 --> 01:15:24.100
and that we're driven by these gods and their dopamine

1710
01:15:26.330 --> 01:15:27.650
and serotonin and oxytocin.

1711
01:15:27.650 --> 01:15:30.000
And there's lots of different things that we do

1712
01:15:32.170 --> 01:15:36.283
to be able to have the right combination of those.

1713
01:15:38.250 --> 01:15:40.443
And some of them are culturally and socially acceptable

1714
01:15:40.443 --> 01:15:41.276
and some of them aren't

1715
01:15:41.276 --> 01:15:45.210
and some are more potentially self-destructive than others.

1716
01:15:45.210 --> 01:15:47.872
We all on some level find our way to the drug

1717
01:15:47.872 --> 01:15:49.257
that works for us.

1718
01:15:49.257 --> 01:15:51.590
And that could be shopping or television,

1719
01:15:51.590 --> 01:15:54.870
or it can be a morphine drip, whatever it is,

1720
01:15:54.870 --> 01:15:58.080
and every combination in between.

1721
01:15:58.080 --> 01:16:00.440
But I think we all gravitate to that thing

1722
01:16:00.440 --> 01:16:04.730
that kind of solves that problem in our brain

1723
01:16:04.730 --> 01:16:06.271
that we feel like we're missing.

1724
01:16:06.271 --> 01:16:10.340
Yeah, and the interesting thing, like for me,

1725
01:16:10.340 --> 01:16:15.340
as I've gone into, I guess just most recently with this,

1726
01:16:15.740 --> 01:16:20.740
just experiencing it, to me it felt as though

1727
01:16:25.820 --> 01:16:28.980
as a misconception, when we look at people

1728
01:16:28.980 --> 01:16:33.470
and think why would so-and-so,

1729
01:16:33.470 --> 01:16:37.450
they have a beautiful family, they have a great job.

1730
01:16:37.450 --> 01:16:39.900
They have a nice house, they have a beautiful life.

1731
01:16:39.900 --> 01:16:44.900
Like why would they choose to use something like that

1732
01:16:44.930 --> 01:16:47.780
when they know how self or how destructive it can be

1733
01:16:51.070 --> 01:16:52.317
as though there's something malicious in it.

1734
01:16:52.317 --> 01:16:54.560
Right, that shit has nothing to do with it.

1735
01:16:54.560 --> 01:16:55.393
Exactly.

1736
01:16:55.393 --> 01:17:00.100
And my view of that, I guess, most recently with this

1737
01:17:03.041 --> 01:17:07.093
the reason that somebody goes to use something like that

1738
01:17:07.093 --> 01:17:09.400
is because they love their life

1739
01:17:09.400 --> 01:17:11.250
because they love their family because they love

1740
01:17:11.250 --> 01:17:13.360
the version of themselves when they're on that.

1741
01:17:13.360 --> 01:17:15.730
And they hate the version of themselves

1742
01:17:15.730 --> 01:17:16.630
when they're not on it,

1743
01:17:16.630 --> 01:17:18.130
because their friends actually don't like

1744
01:17:18.130 --> 01:17:19.470
the version of them when they're not on it,

1745
01:17:19.470 --> 01:17:22.530
because society doesn't respond well to that version,

1746
01:17:22.530 --> 01:17:27.530
that it actually in some situations,

1747
01:17:28.160 --> 01:17:30.170
when you're completely dependent upon it,

1748
01:17:30.170 --> 01:17:31.520
it does make you a better person.

1749
01:17:31.520 --> 01:17:34.460
It does make you more capable of functioning

1750
01:17:34.460 --> 01:17:36.916
within whatever that life that you've created is.

1751
01:17:36.916 --> 01:17:41.916
And gosh, people think about how expensive

1752
01:17:45.260 --> 01:17:46.613
those types of things are,

1753
01:17:47.520 --> 01:17:48.980
and how inconvenient those things are.

1754
01:17:48.980 --> 01:17:52.025
But it's like, gosh, rehab and sobriety

1755
01:17:52.025 --> 01:17:57.025
and coming off of those things, gosh, that's expensive

1756
01:17:57.840 --> 01:18:00.510
and there's never a convenient time for that.

1757
01:18:00.510 --> 01:18:02.433
It will absolutely,

1758
01:18:05.030 --> 01:18:08.701
just rock everything that you have going on.

1759
01:18:08.701 --> 01:18:13.701
I mean, I spent three weeks in a bed just whiling.

1760
01:18:13.750 --> 01:18:15.742
Just trying to detox.

1761
01:18:15.742 --> 01:18:20.497
And just shaking and it felt like my brain,

1762
01:18:21.410 --> 01:18:24.010
my spinal cord, my entire nervous system

1763
01:18:24.010 --> 01:18:26.770
was having an allergic reaction.

1764
01:18:26.770 --> 01:18:28.863
It's like if your brain could itch,

1765
01:18:31.987 --> 01:18:34.297
and just such a strong pull that you know that in it,

1766
01:18:42.529 --> 01:18:44.380
all you have to take is one of these little beautiful

1767
01:18:44.380 --> 01:18:45.530
little white pills.

1768
01:18:45.530 --> 01:18:46.870
And within 10 minutes it's gone

1769
01:18:46.870 --> 01:18:47.819
and you can stop that.

1770
01:18:47.819 --> 01:18:51.170
And you will go to the ends of the earth

1771
01:18:51.170 --> 01:18:52.370
to solve that equation.

1772
01:18:52.370 --> 01:18:53.203
Yeah.

1773
01:18:55.393 --> 01:18:56.279
So heavy, man.

1774
01:18:56.279 --> 01:18:58.080
Yeah, but it's good though,

1775
01:18:58.080 --> 01:19:02.873
it's good to experience that and to just to realize

1776
01:19:09.757 --> 01:19:10.737
there's just such this misconception

1777
01:19:10.737 --> 01:19:13.900
that we have that there's malicious intent.

1778
01:19:13.900 --> 01:19:17.450
That if somebody struggles with something like that,

1779
01:19:17.450 --> 01:19:20.080
it's because they're deviant, they're malevolent,

1780
01:19:20.080 --> 01:19:21.253
it's because they just-

1781
01:19:23.190 --> 01:19:24.970
They're on a search for wholeness

1782
01:19:24.970 --> 01:19:26.870
and that comes in various forms.

1783
01:19:26.870 --> 01:19:29.150
But when you find that drug and you latch onto it,

1784
01:19:29.150 --> 01:19:30.604
it's really hard to let go.

1785
01:19:30.604 --> 01:19:32.120
And you being somebody

1786
01:19:32.120 --> 01:19:35.770
who's already innately very compassionate and empathetic

1787
01:19:35.770 --> 01:19:37.410
towards other human beings

1788
01:19:37.410 --> 01:19:40.010
it just deepens that reservoir of empathy.

1789
01:19:40.010 --> 01:19:44.320
And helps suspend judgment for those that struggle

1790
01:19:44.320 --> 01:19:47.440
and are in pain and in that vicious cycle.

1791
01:19:47.440 --> 01:19:48.273
There you go.

1792
01:19:48.273 --> 01:19:49.106
Yeah, it's hard.

1793
01:19:49.106 --> 01:19:51.710
Well, we glossed over like the diagnosis.

1794
01:19:51.710 --> 01:19:55.780
I mean you have this rare lymphoma,

1795
01:19:55.780 --> 01:20:00.780
but the actual diagnosis is primary pulmonary NKT cell,

1796
01:20:02.066 --> 01:20:02.960
right?

1797
01:20:02.960 --> 01:20:05.780
And this is like a crazy rare thing.

1798
01:20:05.780 --> 01:20:08.480
Like it's surprising that they were able to diagnose you

1799
01:20:08.480 --> 01:20:11.590
because so few people, how many cases are there?

1800
01:20:11.590 --> 01:20:13.012
There's almost no cases.

1801
01:20:13.012 --> 01:20:14.650
Yeah, I don't know.

1802
01:20:14.650 --> 01:20:15.803
Yeah, stay out of that part of that.

1803
01:20:15.803 --> 01:20:19.050
That's the part that's a little bit tricky,

1804
01:20:19.050 --> 01:20:23.280
I start to hear or I start to read about it or research

1805
01:20:23.280 --> 01:20:26.330
or I hear kinda overhear stuff

1806
01:20:26.330 --> 01:20:29.210
and it doesn't bolster my confidence.

1807
01:20:29.210 --> 01:20:30.360
So I stay away from it.

1808
01:20:34.670 --> 01:20:38.563
So in the hospital five months?

1809
01:20:39.500 --> 01:20:40.333
Oh boy.

1810
01:20:41.240 --> 01:20:42.433
Don't even know.

1811
01:20:43.607 --> 01:20:45.023
July through January,

1812
01:20:47.610 --> 01:20:51.660
January, February, March, right, July, August, September,

1813
01:20:51.660 --> 01:20:56.030
October, seven months, yeah seven months or so.

1814
01:20:56.030 --> 01:20:58.740
Steph wrote, she kind of went through the litany

1815
01:20:58.740 --> 01:21:01.130
of all the procedures that you endured.

1816
01:21:01.130 --> 01:21:02.420
Are you aware of these?

1817
01:21:02.420 --> 01:21:04.610
'Cause I was thinking about recounting some of them,

1818
01:21:04.610 --> 01:21:07.960
but if you don't want to be.

1819
01:21:07.960 --> 01:21:11.140
Yeah on a surface level.

1820
01:21:11.140 --> 01:21:13.970
You're aware, you know what they did you.

1821
01:21:13.970 --> 01:21:14.803
Yeah, I think so.

1822
01:21:14.803 --> 01:21:16.280
When they plucked and brought it out of you.

1823
01:21:16.280 --> 01:21:20.009
I mean, basically we don't have to find a point on this,

1824
01:21:20.009 --> 01:21:23.471
but multiple surgeries, most of them while sedated,

1825
01:21:23.471 --> 01:21:26.950
ventilator intubation, which we mentioned,

1826
01:21:26.950 --> 01:21:31.215
open lung biopsy, ECMO placement in both your jugular

1827
01:21:31.215 --> 01:21:35.543
and femoral vein, tracheotomy, PEG GI feeding,

1828
01:21:37.110 --> 01:21:41.263
two chest tubes for collapsed lungs and internal bleeds.

1829
01:21:42.140 --> 01:21:44.370
You suffered acute liver failure.

1830
01:21:44.370 --> 01:21:47.740
You had deep vein thrombosis in your arm.

1831
01:21:47.740 --> 01:21:51.110
You had a stage three sacral pressure ulcer

1832
01:21:51.110 --> 01:21:54.220
and two different strains of fungal lung infections,

1833
01:21:54.220 --> 01:21:57.200
as well as a septic blood infection.

1834
01:21:57.200 --> 01:22:01.170
You suffered from severe ICU and steroid induced delirium.

1835
01:22:01.170 --> 01:22:04.128
Many days you were unable to recognize stuff

1836
01:22:04.128 --> 01:22:06.780
or even remember your own name.

1837
01:22:06.780 --> 01:22:10.710
You lost 70 pounds of muscle going from 170

1838
01:22:10.710 --> 01:22:13.503
down to 98, 95 pounds, something like that.

1839
01:22:15.090 --> 01:22:17.220
You had to relearn how to talk, how to swallow,

1840
01:22:17.220 --> 01:22:22.150
how to chew, you strain but succeeded to speak

1841
01:22:22.150 --> 01:22:26.220
on a Passy-Muir valve, which I don't know what that is

1842
01:22:26.220 --> 01:22:29.720
while still on ECMO and a ventilator.

1843
01:22:29.720 --> 01:22:31.770
You had to relearn how to move your limbs

1844
01:22:31.770 --> 01:22:34.430
beginning with your finger and your toes.

1845
01:22:34.430 --> 01:22:36.160
This took weeks.

1846
01:22:36.160 --> 01:22:38.503
This is insane, it goes on and on and on.

1847
01:22:39.400 --> 01:22:42.960
Do you have memory of these phases and stuff like that?

1848
01:22:42.960 --> 01:22:46.483
Yeah, the surgeries, all the procedures,

1849
01:22:47.418 --> 01:22:48.780
I've got big scars now.

1850
01:22:48.780 --> 01:22:52.120
And I've learned about a lot of this just from

1851
01:22:53.450 --> 01:22:55.750
a lot of it happened when I was, I guess in a coma.

1852
01:22:55.750 --> 01:23:00.750
And so I woke up with all these incisions

1853
01:23:02.810 --> 01:23:05.413
or just with tubes still connected.

1854
01:23:07.202 --> 01:23:10.727
And some of it, the crazy part is that

1855
01:23:11.850 --> 01:23:15.390
some of these procedures I was sedated for,

1856
01:23:15.390 --> 01:23:17.835
but I remember them as they were happening.

1857
01:23:17.835 --> 01:23:22.487
