2115
01:39:30.400 --> 01:39:31.233
They're beautiful.

2116
01:39:31.233 --> 01:39:33.810
I love reading your writing and Steph too.

2117
01:39:33.810 --> 01:39:35.160
Like the two of you together,

2118
01:39:35.160 --> 01:39:37.427
it's like, just write a book together.

2119
01:39:37.427 --> 01:39:38.563
That's incredible.

2120
01:39:39.950 --> 01:39:41.350
Thank you, that means a lot.

2121
01:39:44.690 --> 01:39:46.500
It's always just been my journal, really,

2122
01:39:46.500 --> 01:39:50.440
and from the time I was a kid

2123
01:39:52.740 --> 01:39:54.940
we were encouraged to write every day.

2124
01:39:54.940 --> 01:39:59.050
And my dad has had a journal that he writes in

2125
01:39:59.050 --> 01:40:00.733
and has kept since the 1970s.

2126
01:40:01.827 --> 01:40:04.780
And it's like 17,000 pages long.

2127
01:40:04.780 --> 01:40:05.683
Wow.

2128
01:40:06.940 --> 01:40:11.057
He writes it every month and then prints it out

2129
01:40:13.110 --> 01:40:18.110
and sends it to each of us and it's basically that,

2130
01:40:19.180 --> 01:40:21.320
it's just his thoughts and the things that he reads

2131
01:40:21.320 --> 01:40:22.590
and the things that he experiences.

2132
01:40:22.590 --> 01:40:27.590
And so we were always taught to do that,

2133
01:40:29.310 --> 01:40:31.547
that, that was something that you should do on a daily basis

2134
01:40:31.547 --> 01:40:32.640
is to sit down and write.

2135
01:40:32.640 --> 01:40:37.113
And my little daughter, my oldest of the three,

2136
01:40:39.520 --> 01:40:42.135
she's old enough to where she can do something like that.

2137
01:40:42.135 --> 01:40:43.410
She can sit down and write,

2138
01:40:43.410 --> 01:40:48.410
and that's something that their allowance is based of.

2139
01:40:48.710 --> 01:40:50.520
It's like, if you write every day,

2140
01:40:50.520 --> 01:40:51.974
at the end of the month, you can add all this up.

2141
01:40:51.974 --> 01:40:53.630
Oh, its cool.

2142
01:40:53.630 --> 01:40:57.403
And just because it's a way to express,

2143
01:40:59.210 --> 01:41:01.070
to identify what it is that you're feeling,

2144
01:41:01.070 --> 01:41:04.740
and to be able to put it down on paper.

2145
01:41:04.740 --> 01:41:07.570
And it validates that experience for you.

2146
01:41:07.570 --> 01:41:08.450
And if there's other people

2147
01:41:08.450 --> 01:41:13.450
that it can be helpful to them as well, then it's excuse me.

2148
01:41:15.970 --> 01:41:18.970
And you can do it in a way that's generic enough to where.

2149
01:41:18.970 --> 01:41:20.070
Yeah.

2150
01:41:20.070 --> 01:41:21.850
And that's what that's always been.

2151
01:41:21.850 --> 01:41:23.720
Even before social media,

2152
01:41:23.720 --> 01:41:25.720
that's always just been something that I've done

2153
01:41:25.720 --> 01:41:27.550
and it's something I still do.

2154
01:41:27.550 --> 01:41:29.630
I mean, I don't share a lot of it,

2155
01:41:29.630 --> 01:41:34.570
but getting in the practice of writing,

2156
01:41:37.810 --> 01:41:40.957
I think that there's this quote by Hemingway,

2157
01:41:43.490 --> 01:41:47.900
where he talks about writing one true thing.

2158
01:41:47.900 --> 01:41:51.060
He sits down and he just write one true sentence,

2159
01:41:51.060 --> 01:41:53.272
write the truest sentence that.

2160
01:41:53.272 --> 01:41:55.630
And that's what it is a lot of days.

2161
01:41:55.630 --> 01:41:59.800
And it's not always this yearning

2162
01:41:59.800 --> 01:42:01.440
where I have to sit down and write something,

2163
01:42:01.440 --> 01:42:04.190
otherwise I'm not gonna be able to contain my thoughts.

2164
01:42:04.190 --> 01:42:05.630
It's a practice.

2165
01:42:05.630 --> 01:42:08.370
I sit down and I make myself write one true thing.

2166
01:42:08.370 --> 01:42:11.500
And sometimes I share that and sometimes I don't,

2167
01:42:11.500 --> 01:42:12.800
most of the times I don't.

2168
01:42:14.590 --> 01:42:18.560
But it's really helped me in the last several months,

2169
01:42:18.560 --> 01:42:20.733
as I've been trying to get.

2170
01:42:20.733 --> 01:42:21.700
There are aspects of my memory

2171
01:42:21.700 --> 01:42:22.860
that I don't think I'll ever get back,

2172
01:42:22.860 --> 01:42:27.191
but I feel like I'm finally being able to get my mind back,

2173
01:42:27.191 --> 01:42:29.713
have the ability to think and be able to,

2174
01:42:31.960 --> 01:42:34.952
I guess, process kinda just what's going on around me

2175
01:42:34.952 --> 01:42:39.952
and internalize that, and then be able to metabolize it

2176
01:42:39.954 --> 01:42:42.390
through putting it down on paper.

2177
01:42:42.390 --> 01:42:47.190
Yeah, it has a very beat poet vibe to it, right?

2178
01:42:47.190 --> 01:42:51.610
And it's cool 'cause I think,

2179
01:42:51.610 --> 01:42:54.380
we think of athletes being here

2180
01:42:54.380 --> 01:42:56.740
and then artists being over here.

2181
01:42:56.740 --> 01:42:57.573
Yeah.

2182
01:42:57.573 --> 01:42:58.600
And it's rare

2183
01:42:58.600 --> 01:43:02.090
when these two things come in one package.

2184
01:43:02.090 --> 01:43:07.090
And you see it kind of probably more in the ultra space,

2185
01:43:07.170 --> 01:43:11.700
because that's sort of a more kind of esoteric pursuit,

2186
01:43:11.700 --> 01:43:16.700
and I think is prone to kind of waxing poetic, I suppose.

2187
01:43:19.350 --> 01:43:22.950
But I love it when athletes are kind of channeling

2188
01:43:22.950 --> 01:43:26.790
that innate artistic sensibility that I think we all have.

2189
01:43:26.790 --> 01:43:27.623
When I think of you, I mean,

2190
01:43:27.623 --> 01:43:31.820
I think about Ricky Gates being somebody like that.

2191
01:43:31.820 --> 01:43:33.750
I don't know if you guys know each other.

2192
01:43:33.750 --> 01:43:34.830
You don't, sure, right?

2193
01:43:34.830 --> 01:43:38.183
I mean, you guys have a Vulcan mind meld, yeah.

2194
01:43:39.942 --> 01:43:41.677
No, he's really inspiring.

2195
01:43:41.677 --> 01:43:44.295
Yeah, and it's like notes from the edge.

2196
01:43:44.295 --> 01:43:47.540
We're out here, we're explorers of a certain kind,

2197
01:43:47.540 --> 01:43:52.540
and we're reporting back with what we discover out there,

2198
01:43:53.120 --> 01:43:55.860
and I think it's really cool.

2199
01:43:55.860 --> 01:44:00.860
The other piece to this is this default

2200
01:44:01.200 --> 01:44:04.750
to being service-minded, and I was reading or listening

2201
01:44:04.750 --> 01:44:07.000
to something your brother, Jacob, said.

2202
01:44:07.000 --> 01:44:09.090
And he was talking about when you guys

2203
01:44:09.090 --> 01:44:11.750
were in college and in Hawaii.

2204
01:44:11.750 --> 01:44:15.070
And really immersing yourself

2205
01:44:15.070 --> 01:44:17.340
in the indigenous Hawaiian culture

2206
01:44:17.340 --> 01:44:19.840
and trying to understand the precepts and principles

2207
01:44:19.840 --> 01:44:22.740
that kinda underpin their philosophy.

2208
01:44:22.740 --> 01:44:26.980
The idea of Ohana and Aloha and Mana and all of that,

2209
01:44:26.980 --> 01:44:29.830
but a core principle being this idea of ha,

2210
01:44:29.830 --> 01:44:33.590
this predisposition towards giving,

2211
01:44:33.590 --> 01:44:37.320
and the idea that when you are in a giving spirit,

2212
01:44:37.320 --> 01:44:41.575
that it is there that you find your greatest power.

2213
01:44:41.575 --> 01:44:42.408
Yeah.

2214
01:44:42.408 --> 01:44:44.390
So talk about that a little bit.

2215
01:44:44.390 --> 01:44:45.233
Oh, gosh.

2216
01:44:46.210 --> 01:44:49.650
Yeah, we were out there together for a few years,

2217
01:44:49.650 --> 01:44:53.530
and that was an awesome time on Oahu.

2218
01:44:53.530 --> 01:44:56.270
And we had a professor

2219
01:44:56.270 --> 01:44:58.660
that was the Hawaiian studies professor.

2220
01:44:58.660 --> 01:45:02.590
We both studied, now part of what we both studied

2221
01:45:02.590 --> 01:45:04.403
was cultural anthropology.

2222
01:45:05.480 --> 01:45:07.850
Was part of, I guess, what he ended up studying.

2223
01:45:07.850 --> 01:45:09.530
And then, yeah.

2224
01:45:09.530 --> 01:45:11.860
So we had some classes that we were together.

2225
01:45:13.340 --> 01:45:16.960
Yeah, there was definitely, or components

2226
01:45:16.960 --> 01:45:21.960
of that belief system that just really made a lot of sense.

2227
01:45:26.560 --> 01:45:29.600
And I think some of it had to do with our upbringing,

2228
01:45:29.600 --> 01:45:34.320
but this notion that, one of the strong ones

2229
01:45:34.320 --> 01:45:37.650
was this relationship that we have with the natural world,

2230
01:45:37.650 --> 01:45:40.250
with the earth and there's a belief

2231
01:45:40.250 --> 01:45:44.710
within a traditional Hawaiian belief

2232
01:45:44.710 --> 01:45:48.680
that if you are steward of the land,

2233
01:45:48.680 --> 01:45:51.760
if you protect the earth and you care for the earth,

2234
01:45:51.760 --> 01:45:56.720
that the earth has a moral obligation

2235
01:45:56.720 --> 01:45:58.722
to care for you in return.

2236
01:45:58.722 --> 01:46:03.722
And I wish I understood it better,

2237
01:46:06.530 --> 01:46:10.900
but I feel there is a definitely is a connection,

2238
01:46:10.900 --> 01:46:12.343
and there's a sense of,

2239
01:46:14.325 --> 01:46:18.120
there's some type of reciprocal relationship that exists

2240
01:46:18.120 --> 01:46:23.120
where not just in that, well,

2241
01:46:23.317 --> 01:46:27.210
that respiratory relationship that we have with the earth.

2242
01:46:27.210 --> 01:46:30.300
I mean, that's incredible to think about that.

2243
01:46:30.300 --> 01:46:35.300
That we consume carbohydrates,

2244
01:46:35.620 --> 01:46:39.320
and we breathe in oxygen and we drink water.

2245
01:46:39.320 --> 01:46:41.620
And that the combination of those three things

2246
01:46:44.240 --> 01:46:47.860
creates CO2 and energy.

2247
01:46:47.860 --> 01:46:49.537
And then we breathe out that CO2,

2248
01:46:49.537 --> 01:46:52.980
and the plants breathe in that CO2.

2249
01:46:52.980 --> 01:46:53.813
Right.

2250
01:46:53.813 --> 01:46:57.760
And they create carbohydrates and oxygen,

2251
01:46:57.760 --> 01:46:59.060
and breathe that back out.

2252
01:47:01.410 --> 01:47:04.730
And I feel as though there's something else that exists

2253
01:47:09.770 --> 01:47:13.580
that are somehow our connection with the natural world

2254
01:47:13.580 --> 01:47:15.800
and moving through the natural world,

2255
01:47:15.800 --> 01:47:19.130
whether it's walking or running or cycling

2256
01:47:19.130 --> 01:47:21.080
or swimming or surfing, whatever it is.

2257
01:47:24.280 --> 01:47:29.280
The emotions that we store absorb from,

2258
01:47:31.910 --> 01:47:34.843
I've always absorbed people's emotions around me.

2259
01:47:37.209 --> 01:47:39.409
And that gets heavy, that gets really heavy.

2260
01:47:40.450 --> 01:47:44.300
And running has become, it was always an outlet.

2261
01:47:44.300 --> 01:47:47.280
It was always a way to metabolize that emotion

2262
01:47:47.280 --> 01:47:51.570
and somehow breathe it back into the natural world.

2263
01:47:51.570 --> 01:47:53.860
And it felt as though there was something

2264
01:47:53.860 --> 01:47:57.010
within that natural world that I wasn't,

2265
01:47:57.010 --> 01:47:58.770
it wasn't a toxin that I was admitting.

2266
01:47:58.770 --> 01:48:00.773
It was almost as though it was something

2267
01:48:00.773 --> 01:48:03.993
that was beneficial somehow, and.

2268
01:48:05.359 --> 01:48:08.180
Right, the more that you grow and mature

2269
01:48:08.180 --> 01:48:13.180
and metabolize all of this, whatever you're aspirating out

2270
01:48:13.230 --> 01:48:15.890
is providing sustenance to something, yeah.

2271
01:48:15.890 --> 01:48:18.510
Yeah, and the reason that I feel that

2272
01:48:18.510 --> 01:48:23.310
is because I can go out and I can spin my wheels

2273
01:48:23.310 --> 01:48:27.550
and go through the motions in a densely populated city,

2274
01:48:27.550 --> 01:48:30.600
and I don't feel that release,

2275
01:48:30.600 --> 01:48:34.120
I don't feel as though it's metabolizing the same way

2276
01:48:34.120 --> 01:48:39.120
as if I was in in the mountains or in the canyon

2277
01:48:39.467 --> 01:48:42.550
or these hills around here.

2278
01:48:42.550 --> 01:48:44.330
I mean, that's what I love about.

2279
01:48:44.330 --> 01:48:45.420
What I love about here

2280
01:48:45.420 --> 01:48:48.430
is that you can be in such a densely populated area,

2281
01:48:48.430 --> 01:48:49.490
and then you just move.

2282
01:48:49.490 --> 01:48:52.840
Just next door is this rugged wild mountain,

2283
01:48:52.840 --> 01:48:54.370
and you have the ability

2284
01:48:54.370 --> 01:48:58.723
to exhale all of that that you've absorbed.

2285
01:49:00.560 --> 01:49:04.720
Anyway, it goes back to the, I guess,

2286
01:49:04.720 --> 01:49:05.610
the things that we learned there

2287
01:49:05.610 --> 01:49:08.920
when we were in school there,

2288
01:49:08.920 --> 01:49:12.770
and just that this understanding that there is a connection,

2289
01:49:12.770 --> 01:49:14.150
that there's a reciprocal connection,

2290
01:49:14.150 --> 01:49:16.530
not just between us in the natural world,

2291
01:49:16.530 --> 01:49:19.783
but between ourselves and other human beings, and.

2292
01:49:20.830 --> 01:49:23.360
I feel like that's heightened in Hawaii.

2293
01:49:23.360 --> 01:49:26.510
There is a real, palpable sense.

2294
01:49:26.510 --> 01:49:28.411
It's very experiential when you're there,

2295
01:49:28.411 --> 01:49:30.174
because there's a lot of light,

2296
01:49:30.174 --> 01:49:31.540
but there's a lot of dark there.

2297
01:49:31.540 --> 01:49:34.580
There's a polarity that exists for some reason.

2298
01:49:34.580 --> 01:49:36.120
Maybe it's on some Meridian.

2299
01:49:36.120 --> 01:49:37.310
I don't know what's going on there,

2300
01:49:37.310 --> 01:49:39.787
but I mean, it's real as far as I can tell.

2301
01:49:39.787 --> 01:49:41.040
Yeah, absolutely.

2302
01:49:41.040 --> 01:49:43.890
But the trick is then to come back to the main land

2303
01:49:43.890 --> 01:49:47.170
where it's less pronounced

2304
01:49:47.170 --> 01:49:49.900
and maintain that kind of reverence

2305
01:49:49.900 --> 01:49:52.947
and respect and awareness.

2306
01:49:52.947 --> 01:49:55.700
Yeah, and the one thing that I,

2307
01:49:55.700 --> 01:49:56.533
well, not the one thing,

2308
01:49:56.533 --> 01:50:00.210
but one thing that I do love about being out there

2309
01:50:00.210 --> 01:50:04.510
is that the currency out there

2310
01:50:04.510 --> 01:50:09.190
is service to the people around.

2311
01:50:09.190 --> 01:50:11.060
Right, I mean, that's where I was driving at

2312
01:50:11.060 --> 01:50:12.630
with this idea of ha.

2313
01:50:12.630 --> 01:50:13.490
Right, exactly.

2314
01:50:13.490 --> 01:50:16.560
Yeah, I think about the word inspire.

2315
01:50:19.350 --> 01:50:24.350
Inspire means to breathe life into something

2316
01:50:24.550 --> 01:50:26.823
the same as that notion of ha,

2317
01:50:28.790 --> 01:50:32.150
conspire to breathe life into something together

2318
01:50:32.150 --> 01:50:37.150
with somebody else and just that sense

2319
01:50:38.980 --> 01:50:43.980
that you have the ability as a human being

2320
01:50:44.580 --> 01:50:48.840
to positively or negatively affect the people around you,

2321
01:50:48.840 --> 01:50:52.480
based off of the way that you choose to interact

2322
01:50:52.480 --> 01:50:57.480
and our purpose, if there's a purpose,

2323
01:50:57.610 --> 01:51:02.610
our purpose for being here it's to serve each other

2324
01:51:03.220 --> 01:51:08.220
is to try to make the life of another individual easier

2325
01:51:10.200 --> 01:51:11.410
to try to help them on their way

2326
01:51:11.410 --> 01:51:12.839
to try to somehow be of assistance.

2327
01:51:12.839 --> 01:51:17.839
And there's this notion, this need for cooperation

2328
01:51:18.480 --> 01:51:23.340
and coordination that we have as human beings.

2329
01:51:23.340 --> 01:51:25.300
It's existed for hundreds of thousands of years.

2330
01:51:25.300 --> 01:51:30.300
But in the last couple of centuries

2331
01:51:31.040 --> 01:51:34.707
we've become more individualistic,

2332
01:51:34.707 --> 01:51:39.707
we've become more isolated rather than working together

2333
01:51:41.840 --> 01:51:46.083
with individuals cooperating in a tribe setting.

2334
01:51:48.250 --> 01:51:50.190
There's something uniquely American about that

2335
01:51:50.190 --> 01:51:53.850
because of the way we over prioritize

2336
01:51:53.850 --> 01:51:57.270
and over index and kind of Harold

2337
01:51:57.270 --> 01:51:59.480
the idea of rugged individualism,

2338
01:51:59.480 --> 01:52:01.000
which is really an illusion,

2339
01:52:01.000 --> 01:52:04.120
like nobody gets anywhere without cooperation and help.

2340
01:52:04.120 --> 01:52:07.400
But for some reason, we celebrate this idea

2341
01:52:07.400 --> 01:52:12.400
of the self-made man and these sort of principles upon

2342
01:52:12.520 --> 01:52:14.470
which our country was built.

2343
01:52:14.470 --> 01:52:17.610
Really look to that idea of individualism

2344
01:52:17.610 --> 01:52:22.610
as something that stands above all these other kind of ideas

2345
01:52:23.780 --> 01:52:25.640
that I think we should be celebrating

2346
01:52:25.640 --> 01:52:27.205
probably a little bit more

2347
01:52:27.205 --> 01:52:32.205
And as a result, we're isolating ourselves from each other

2348
01:52:33.100 --> 01:52:36.480
and we're not raising our children in a village

2349
01:52:36.480 --> 01:52:37.713
and we don't know our neighbors

2350
01:52:37.713 --> 01:52:41.340
and we consider it a weakness to ask for help.

2351
01:52:41.340 --> 01:52:45.210
And all of these things I think are really denigrating

2352
01:52:45.210 --> 01:52:46.500
the quality of our lives.

2353
01:52:46.500 --> 01:52:47.713
Yeah, absolutely.

2354
01:52:48.584 --> 01:52:53.584
Rather than focusing on coordination and cooperation

2355
01:52:54.190 --> 01:52:59.190
and receiving the reward that comes from that,

2356
01:53:00.660 --> 01:53:04.949
which is this production and distribution of oxytocin,

2357
01:53:04.949 --> 01:53:09.872
we use our consumption and accumulation

2358
01:53:09.872 --> 01:53:12.210
to feed that same need.

2359
01:53:12.210 --> 01:53:15.950
And we isolate ourselves and we get our validation

2360
01:53:15.950 --> 01:53:17.300
through our devices.

2361
01:53:17.300 --> 01:53:20.410
Right, or we only conspire and cooperate

2362
01:53:20.410 --> 01:53:25.410
in order to get to place where we can consume more

2363
01:53:25.530 --> 01:53:28.154
and separate ourselves from our brothers and sisters.

2364
01:53:28.154 --> 01:53:30.237
(laughs)

2365
01:53:32.430 --> 01:53:33.930
I'm sure you have this same experience,

2366
01:53:33.930 --> 01:53:38.930
but we originally met because we were both

2367
01:53:39.310 --> 01:53:43.320
with James the Iron Cowboy on the final day

2368
01:53:43.320 --> 01:53:44.930
of his 50, 50, 50,

2369
01:53:44.930 --> 01:53:46.550
and we got to run a few miles together

2370
01:53:46.550 --> 01:53:47.970
and that was super fun.

2371
01:53:47.970 --> 01:53:51.550
And one of the things I remember about that experience

2372
01:53:51.550 --> 01:53:55.729
was you being not just a cheerleader,

2373
01:53:55.729 --> 01:53:58.280
you were there to serve, like you're running back and forth.

2374
01:53:58.280 --> 01:53:59.650
You're making sure everybody's okay.

2375
01:53:59.650 --> 01:54:01.990
You're making sure James is okay.

2376
01:54:01.990 --> 01:54:06.530
And I was very aware of kind of,

2377
01:54:06.530 --> 01:54:10.320
I was very conscious of the intentionality

2378
01:54:10.320 --> 01:54:12.600
that you brought to that experience

2379
01:54:12.600 --> 01:54:15.880
in that it was all about making sure

2380
01:54:15.880 --> 01:54:18.230
it was the best day ever for James, right?

2381
01:54:18.230 --> 01:54:19.063
Yeah.

2382
01:54:19.063 --> 01:54:20.000
And it was, and it was amazing.

2383
01:54:20.000 --> 01:54:21.563
And I'll never forget that day.

2384
01:54:22.750 --> 01:54:26.960
I went back to on two occasions to participate

2385
01:54:28.900 --> 01:54:32.420
in his latest crazy adventure, conquer 101,

2386
01:54:32.420 --> 01:54:34.170
that's what you call it, right?

2387
01:54:34.170 --> 01:54:35.840
And one of the things,

2388
01:54:35.840 --> 01:54:39.903
set aside the sheer athletic achievement,

2389
01:54:40.900 --> 01:54:43.650
the thing that was most meaningful to me

2390
01:54:43.650 --> 01:54:47.790
was seeing this whole town turn up to support this guy,

2391
01:54:47.790 --> 01:54:50.010
like a small little town, not really a suburb,

2392
01:54:50.010 --> 01:54:51.880
like it's far enough away from salt lake

2393
01:54:51.880 --> 01:54:56.553
that it's kind of its own place with its own community.

2394
01:54:58.350 --> 01:55:01.260
And everybody showed up to support this guy.

2395
01:55:01.260 --> 01:55:04.040
And all these people flew in from all over the place

2396
01:55:04.040 --> 01:55:05.328
to support this guy.

2397
01:55:05.328 --> 01:55:10.328
And it was the most kind of cooperative situation.

2398
01:55:11.340 --> 01:55:14.290
And in some ways, kind of a throwback to a different time,

2399
01:55:14.290 --> 01:55:16.330
because it's a place where kids ride their bikes

2400
01:55:16.330 --> 01:55:19.644
down the street and everybody says hello to each other

2401
01:55:19.644 --> 01:55:22.700
it's a very leave it to beaver kind of town, right?

2402
01:55:22.700 --> 01:55:23.570
Yeah, absolutely.

2403
01:55:23.570 --> 01:55:26.033
And I was like, wow, this is kinda nice.

2404
01:55:27.170 --> 01:55:30.270
I live in LA, I don't see this anywhere, right?

2405
01:55:30.270 --> 01:55:34.370
But it is that spirit of cooperation

2406
01:55:34.370 --> 01:55:37.871
and a sensibility around kind of giving of yourself

2407
01:55:37.871 --> 01:55:40.370
or something greater than yourself.

2408
01:55:40.370 --> 01:55:41.490
Yeah, and its been a-.

2409
01:55:41.490 --> 01:55:44.410
And I know you went out there for I think I went out there

2410
01:55:44.410 --> 01:55:46.050
on the last day and James was telling me

2411
01:55:46.050 --> 01:55:46.950
that you had been out there,

2412
01:55:46.950 --> 01:55:48.640
I think a couple of days prior.

2413
01:55:48.640 --> 01:55:52.298
Yeah, I got there in New Jersey.

2414
01:55:52.298 --> 01:55:54.703
So I think the second half of maybe the last like 25 or so.

2415
01:55:55.650 --> 01:55:56.483
Right on the 50, yeah.

2416
01:55:56.483 --> 01:55:58.520
I mean, you were there for a huge long stretch,

2417
01:55:58.520 --> 01:56:01.780
but I mean the latest thing you were out,

2418
01:56:01.780 --> 01:56:02.990
you came out for a day.

2419
01:56:02.990 --> 01:56:06.570
Yeah, if I was my dad and my sister,

2420
01:56:06.570 --> 01:56:08.223
and we went out to see him.

2421
01:56:09.630 --> 01:56:10.830
Gosh, that's incredible.

2422
01:56:12.268 --> 01:56:13.300
There's not even words.

2423
01:56:13.300 --> 01:56:15.510
What do you even say about it?

2424
01:56:15.510 --> 01:56:16.587
Well, yeah.

2425
01:56:16.587 --> 01:56:19.683
And James is, gosh, his ability to,

2426
01:56:23.840 --> 01:56:27.180
just exactly what we were talking about earlier,

2427
01:56:27.180 --> 01:56:30.210
the potential that we have as human beings

2428
01:56:30.210 --> 01:56:35.210
to explore those outer ragged edges of that human capability

2429
01:56:35.260 --> 01:56:39.710
and to do it in a way that just empowers other people

2430
01:56:41.290 --> 01:56:43.970
to realize what it is that we're actually capable of,

2431
01:56:43.970 --> 01:56:48.780
to rewrite those rules and the way that he is able to do it

2432
01:56:51.242 --> 01:56:54.633
is through that cooperation and coordination.

2433
01:56:56.300 --> 01:57:01.300
Excuse me, to see how, it is an equal distribution of effort

2434
01:57:03.538 --> 01:57:06.060
by him and Sunny and the kids.

2435
01:57:06.060 --> 01:57:09.890
Sure, and he's the first person to make sure

2436
01:57:09.890 --> 01:57:12.440
that everybody understands that this is a team.

2437
01:57:12.440 --> 01:57:13.273
Yeah, exactly.

2438
01:57:14.810 --> 01:57:17.010
The wing man are unbelievable.

2439
01:57:17.010 --> 01:57:20.670
Yeah, they are, gosh, yeah that's a good crew.

2440
01:57:20.670 --> 01:57:22.960
I mean, that's a solid group of people

2441
01:57:22.960 --> 01:57:27.027
and you can't help but walk away from that

2442
01:57:27.027 --> 01:57:32.027
and be just inspired by, you know what I mean,

2443
01:57:32.167 --> 01:57:34.950
the same way that I mentioned

2444
01:57:34.950 --> 01:57:36.640
that it breathes life into you,

2445
01:57:36.640 --> 01:57:39.220
it helps you realize what you're capable of

2446
01:57:39.220 --> 01:57:41.300
as a human and not just what James has done,

2447
01:57:41.300 --> 01:57:46.300
but just like the way that we can rally around any cause

2448
01:57:46.900 --> 01:57:47.733
whatever it is.

2449
01:57:47.733 --> 01:57:52.733
And even not just the outward things that are said,

2450
01:57:52.790 --> 01:57:54.410
but just observing it, just watching it,

2451
01:57:54.410 --> 01:57:56.631
just seeing how it plays out and doing it so transparently

2452
01:57:56.631 --> 01:57:59.050
and openly like that to where everybody sees

2453
01:58:00.878 --> 01:58:02.964
there's no curtain that separates behind the scenes

2454
01:58:02.964 --> 01:58:04.090
in the front stage.

2455
01:58:04.090 --> 01:58:05.620
Everybody is just right out there

2456
01:58:05.620 --> 01:58:10.620
and you see all of it and you realize how much it takes

2457
01:58:12.330 --> 01:58:13.918
to be able to pull something like that off.

2458
01:58:13.918 --> 01:58:16.220
And that makes it even more inspiring

2459
01:58:16.220 --> 01:58:21.220
just that you can create something like that,

2460
01:58:21.270 --> 01:58:23.943
a community, a tribe, a family, whatever it is.

2461
01:58:26.870 --> 01:58:28.970
You have a goal and then you figure out a plan

2462
01:58:28.970 --> 01:58:31.730
and then you just chip away at it until you get it.

2463
01:58:31.730 --> 01:58:32.563
I know.

2464
01:58:32.563 --> 01:58:34.040
It's pretty incredible.

2465
01:58:34.040 --> 01:58:38.320
In your case, so much of who you are,

2466
01:58:38.320 --> 01:58:41.810
so much of your identity is that of the athlete, right?

2467
01:58:41.810 --> 01:58:45.630
And now obviously you have to recalibrate that whole thing.

2468
01:58:45.630 --> 01:58:46.790
How has that been for you

2469
01:58:46.790 --> 01:58:49.430
and how do you think about that?

2470
01:58:49.430 --> 01:58:52.713
Oh man, in my head it doesn't change.

2471
01:59:04.170 --> 01:59:06.250
I've had the opportunity to be around

2472
01:59:06.250 --> 01:59:08.630
a lot of really, really great athletes

2473
01:59:09.900 --> 01:59:14.900
living in Flagstaff working and being a body mechanic,

2474
01:59:18.180 --> 01:59:21.080
basically working on bodies, studying physical therapy,

2475
01:59:21.080 --> 01:59:25.670
studying massage, studying manual therapy,

2476
01:59:25.670 --> 01:59:28.520
you have the opportunity to be around

2477
01:59:28.520 --> 01:59:32.743
and put your hands on lots of really incredible athletes.

2478
01:59:35.340 --> 01:59:37.630
And it's really good because it helps you realize

2479
01:59:37.630 --> 01:59:42.630
how remarkable you are, you know and you go to Flagstaff,

2480
01:59:43.550 --> 01:59:46.562
you learn really quick that you're a tiny fish

2481
01:59:46.562 --> 01:59:48.630
in an enormous pond.

2482
01:59:48.630 --> 01:59:51.220
Oh, there's Ryan Hall or Sarah Hall.

2483
01:59:51.220 --> 01:59:52.490
There's Scott Falls.

2484
01:59:52.490 --> 01:59:57.403
Exactly, yeah, even on the buildups

2485
01:59:59.210 --> 02:00:01.066
where I was in the very best shape of my life,

2486
02:00:01.066 --> 02:00:04.470
there are three dozen males and females

2487
02:00:08.431 --> 02:00:13.431
that could kick my ass on any distance, in any event.

2488
02:00:18.458 --> 02:00:22.360
And I was just never going to be the greatest.

2489
02:00:22.360 --> 02:00:24.690
I was never going to be a great, great athlete.

2490
02:00:24.690 --> 02:00:29.410
I like to do everything that I could to wring out

2491
02:00:29.410 --> 02:00:34.410
my own potential, but I was very, very aware

2492
02:00:34.760 --> 02:00:36.210
that I was never going to be,

2493
02:00:38.836 --> 02:00:41.030
I'm not built to be a distance runner.

2494
02:00:41.030 --> 02:00:43.250
Sure, but at the same time, there's that,

2495
02:00:43.250 --> 02:00:45.774
there's the kind of performance outcomes

2496
02:00:45.774 --> 02:00:47.470
of being an athlete,

2497
02:00:47.470 --> 02:00:50.350
but more importantly or more meaningfully

2498
02:00:50.350 --> 02:00:52.750
is just the experience of being an athlete.

2499
02:00:52.750 --> 02:00:54.079
Like I get to go out and run these trails

2500
02:00:54.079 --> 02:00:58.060
and I can feel a certain way and run these distances

2501
02:00:58.060 --> 02:00:59.490
and connect with my breath.

2502
02:00:59.490 --> 02:01:04.140
And all of that, that gives us sustenance, right?

2503
02:01:04.140 --> 02:01:06.659
Is suddenly not available to you.

2504
02:01:06.659 --> 02:01:10.270
I guess what I was getting at is that it is still,

2505
02:01:10.270 --> 02:01:11.270
it's just different.

2506
02:01:11.270 --> 02:01:16.183
And it's just the thing that drew me to it was always,

2507
02:01:17.780 --> 02:01:18.920
be a part of that community,

2508
02:01:18.920 --> 02:01:23.920
being able to feel a part of, there's this cohesion

2509
02:01:24.298 --> 02:01:28.270
that takes place when you're out suffering alongside people

2510
02:01:28.270 --> 02:01:30.070
and training towards something and working collectively

2511
02:01:30.070 --> 02:01:30.903
towards something.

2512
02:01:30.903 --> 02:01:35.903
And my body is wrecked.

2513
02:01:36.182 --> 02:01:41.182
It likely won't ever be able to recover fully,

2514
02:01:43.330 --> 02:01:45.210
but I can still get out.

2515
02:01:45.210 --> 02:01:47.132
Yeah, I was gonna ask you like what can you do?

2516
02:01:47.132 --> 02:01:48.680
What does it look like?

2517
02:01:48.680 --> 02:01:51.420
Well, it's a lot of walking right now,

2518
02:01:51.420 --> 02:01:53.510
especially in Flagstaff at that elevation

2519
02:01:54.573 --> 02:01:56.950
and I'll get out and just slowly move.

2520
02:01:56.950 --> 02:02:00.410
And just that process of moving through the natural world,

2521
02:02:00.410 --> 02:02:03.210
it still provides the same.

2522
02:02:03.210 --> 02:02:07.819
I would have never loved, it's fun to compete,

2523
02:02:07.819 --> 02:02:12.819
but I really have always done it as a way of just being able

2524
02:02:17.170 --> 02:02:20.540
to work through my own issues.

2525
02:02:20.540 --> 02:02:23.160
It's this meditative reverent practice

2526
02:02:23.160 --> 02:02:27.110
of going out and I guess seeking

2527
02:02:28.110 --> 02:02:31.900
this transcendental experience and you become

2528
02:02:31.900 --> 02:02:32.760
so dependent upon it,

2529
02:02:32.760 --> 02:02:34.860
you have to make a lifestyle out of it eventually,

2530
02:02:34.860 --> 02:02:39.860
otherwise it gets flagged as something that's an addiction.

2531
02:02:39.860 --> 02:02:41.834
It's self destructive.

2532
02:02:41.834 --> 02:02:43.170
Let me just shroud all of this

2533
02:02:43.170 --> 02:02:44.920
and tell everybody it's a career.

2534
02:02:44.920 --> 02:02:47.877
If you can do that, but that's what it is.

2535
02:02:47.877 --> 02:02:52.877
That really is otherwise it becomes,

2536
02:02:54.550 --> 02:02:58.293
something that people say you should probably reconsider.

2537
02:02:58.293 --> 02:03:00.987
I remember I was talking to my dad a couple of years ago,

2538
02:03:00.987 --> 02:03:02.540
and my dad is like my best friend.

2539
02:03:02.540 --> 02:03:05.183
We talk on the phone like almost every day.

2540
02:03:09.820 --> 02:03:11.970
I was out on the same road, the same road that I run

2541
02:03:11.970 --> 02:03:14.610
back and forth day after day after day.

2542
02:03:14.610 --> 02:03:17.350
And I think it was like the third run of the day,

2543
02:03:17.350 --> 02:03:18.590
just because I'd had to break it up

2544
02:03:18.590 --> 02:03:21.250
and I was getting ready for a marathon.

2545
02:03:21.250 --> 02:03:24.520
And I'd been living in Flagstaff for, I dunno,

2546
02:03:24.520 --> 02:03:29.400
maybe six years and pursuing that full-time

2547
02:03:29.400 --> 02:03:33.387
and he said, "Wow, you're running again here,

2548
02:03:33.387 --> 02:03:34.620
you're the end of this running thing actually.

2549
02:03:34.620 --> 02:03:37.213
I was like, "Yes, dad."

2550
02:03:38.730 --> 02:03:42.550
It's something that I am pursuing a little bit seriously.

2551
02:03:42.550 --> 02:03:45.480
And I was like, dad, that'd be like, if I said to you like,

2552
02:03:45.480 --> 02:03:46.313
oh, you're still reading books.

2553
02:03:46.313 --> 02:03:49.360
Oh you're really into reading thing aren't you?

2554
02:03:49.360 --> 02:03:50.762
Still writing in your journal?

2555
02:03:50.762 --> 02:03:55.762
Yeah, exactly, yeah.

2556
02:03:55.870 --> 02:03:57.800
It's changed, it's different, it's slower,

2557
02:03:57.800 --> 02:04:00.527
but gosh, if we got to the end of this life

2558
02:04:00.527 --> 02:04:03.465
and if there was some kind of sit down judgment,

2559
02:04:03.465 --> 02:04:07.150
reconciliation, and the only thing that you had to show

2560
02:04:07.150 --> 02:04:10.122
for it was how fast you could run, what a waste of a life

2561
02:04:10.122 --> 02:04:11.289
that would be.

2562
02:04:12.675 --> 02:04:14.870
It's like, well, what did you do?

2563
02:04:14.870 --> 02:04:17.832
It's like, well, did you see how fast I could ran,

2564
02:04:17.832 --> 02:04:18.987
did you see how fast I could run a marathon?

2565
02:04:18.987 --> 02:04:21.960
And that is a pretty pathetic existence, you know?

2566
02:04:25.180 --> 02:04:28.373
There's nothing more self-serving than that, you know?

2567
02:04:31.470 --> 02:04:34.160
And so yeah, I don't know, who knows.

2568
02:04:34.160 --> 02:04:36.010
I don't know if I'll ever run that fast again.

2569
02:04:36.010 --> 02:04:37.540
I mean you've shared on Instagram,

2570
02:04:37.540 --> 02:04:40.750
you were out in the canyon with your daughter,

2571
02:04:40.750 --> 02:04:42.953
so you're getting out there in your own.

2572
02:04:43.970 --> 02:04:47.900
Yeah, I get out and move and that's as good as it gets,

2573
02:04:47.900 --> 02:04:51.180
being able to be in the canyon with Harper.

2574
02:04:51.180 --> 02:04:53.573
She's 11, she's incredible.

2575
02:04:55.040 --> 02:04:59.310
She says things that the way that she's connected

2576
02:04:59.310 --> 02:05:04.201
to the natural world it's unlike anything

2577
02:05:04.201 --> 02:05:07.723
I've ever experienced, we're walking and she said.

2578
02:05:09.630 --> 02:05:13.323
She said, "Dad, it sounds like the way that,

2579
02:05:14.330 --> 02:05:16.530
you know when you lick a rock?"

2580
02:05:16.530 --> 02:05:18.360
And I said, "What?"

2581
02:05:18.360 --> 02:05:20.380
She goes, "It's the sound of licking a rock,

2582
02:05:20.380 --> 02:05:23.550
but not like a river rock and not a piece of limestone,

2583
02:05:23.550 --> 02:05:24.990
like a piece of sandstone.

2584
02:05:24.990 --> 02:05:25.923
Do you know that sound that it makes

2585
02:05:25.923 --> 02:05:27.988
when you lick a piece of sandstone?"

2586
02:05:27.988 --> 02:05:28.821
And I was like,

2587
02:05:28.821 --> 02:05:30.870
"I know exactly what you're talking about."

2588
02:05:30.870 --> 02:05:31.793
That kinda.

2589
02:05:33.460 --> 02:05:35.970
There's the poet, that's amazing.

2590
02:05:35.970 --> 02:05:37.320
No, I was just like, wow.

2591
02:05:40.809 --> 02:05:41.990
That's so in tune.

2592
02:05:41.990 --> 02:05:42.823
Right.

2593
02:05:42.823 --> 02:05:43.656
Having experiences like that with her.

2594
02:05:43.656 --> 02:05:47.413
And does she like shame, somebody had like a boom box

2595
02:05:47.413 --> 02:05:49.400
or something like that down in the canyon.

2596
02:05:49.400 --> 02:05:52.310
And she was like, just not into it.

2597
02:05:52.310 --> 02:05:53.143
Yeah.

2598
02:05:53.143 --> 02:05:57.580
She just shook her head she goes, oh, noise pollution.

2599
02:05:57.580 --> 02:05:58.413
And I was like, yeah.

2600
02:05:58.413 --> 02:06:00.200
And it wasn't even to me, it was just to herself

2601
02:06:00.200 --> 02:06:01.033
and I just heard it.

2602
02:06:01.033 --> 02:06:02.430
And I was like, man, that's awesome.

2603
02:06:02.430 --> 02:06:04.010
That's a really proud moment.

2604
02:06:04.010 --> 02:06:06.680
Then she said something to me and it reminded me

2605
02:06:06.680 --> 02:06:08.710
of something that maybe I had said to her at one point,

2606
02:06:08.710 --> 02:06:10.870
but she said it back to me as though

2607
02:06:10.870 --> 02:06:12.420
it was an independent thought

2608
02:06:12.420 --> 02:06:14.320
I could tell it was like something that was a part of her,

2609
02:06:14.320 --> 02:06:17.501
but I was having a hard time coming back out,

2610
02:06:17.501 --> 02:06:18.830
we're hiking back out.

2611
02:06:18.830 --> 02:06:21.140
And gosh, I swear the canyon got wasted

2612
02:06:21.140 --> 02:06:24.000
for the last year and a half.

2613
02:06:25.434 --> 02:06:27.350
I'm coming back out and she's like, hey, you know what?

2614
02:06:27.350 --> 02:06:28.632
It's okay, like, it's okay.

2615
02:06:28.632 --> 02:06:33.632
Sometimes the memory is a lot better

2616
02:06:34.920 --> 02:06:37.930
than when we're actually living the experience.

2617
02:06:37.930 --> 02:06:40.393
And I was like, gosh, this kid.

2618
02:06:41.960 --> 02:06:44.590
Children are our teachers, Tommy.

2619
02:06:44.590 --> 02:06:46.540
That's unbelievable, wow.

2620
02:06:46.540 --> 02:06:49.097
To be able to have that experience.

2621
02:06:49.097 --> 02:06:50.930
And the fact that I can do that now,

2622
02:06:50.930 --> 02:06:52.230
be able to walk down there with her

2623
02:06:52.230 --> 02:06:56.217
and be able to walk in the trails just around her home

2624
02:06:56.217 --> 02:07:00.090
and sit outside and play with the ducks

2625
02:07:00.090 --> 02:07:02.310
and the chickens and just all of that.

2626
02:07:02.310 --> 02:07:06.576
It's like there's nothing better than that.

2627
02:07:06.576 --> 02:07:10.150
And I have that now, I remember a year ago,

2628
02:07:10.150 --> 02:07:13.280
a year and a half ago, bartering with whoever it is,

2629
02:07:13.280 --> 02:07:15.843
that is in charge of all of this.

2630
02:07:17.180 --> 02:07:18.550
Just let me get through this year,

2631
02:07:18.550 --> 02:07:19.810
just give me one more year.

2632
02:07:19.810 --> 02:07:24.810
If I can have a year, and then I'll gladly go

2633
02:07:24.820 --> 02:07:27.023
and I've made it, it's been a year.

2634
02:07:29.800 --> 02:07:30.940
Gosh, and then you get selfish.

2635
02:07:30.940 --> 02:07:33.533
You're like, okay, five more, 20 more, 50 more.

2636
02:07:33.533 --> 02:07:35.080
'Cause we're humans, man, right?

2637
02:07:35.080 --> 02:07:36.230
Yeah, exactly,

2638
02:07:36.230 --> 02:07:39.866
but it doesn't get any better than this.

2639
02:07:39.866 --> 02:07:41.030
It really doesn't.

2640
02:07:41.030 --> 02:07:43.893
And I mean, the tragedy is that we don't see it

2641
02:07:47.980 --> 02:07:50.253
until afterwards, you know?

2642
02:07:50.253 --> 02:07:55.253
I've been thinking about people say like,

2643
02:07:55.920 --> 02:08:00.222
and they lived happily ever after,

2644
02:08:00.222 --> 02:08:02.903
the operative verb there is lived.

2645
02:08:03.768 --> 02:08:08.768
They were alive and that's why it was happily ever after

2646
02:08:12.790 --> 02:08:15.600
then they died and it becomes a cooking tragedy

2647
02:08:22.670 --> 02:08:23.823
life is incredible.

2648
02:08:27.250 --> 02:08:29.573
As bad as it seems, as hard as it seems,

2649
02:08:32.260 --> 02:08:36.980
it's just such a gift to be able to do it.

2650
02:08:36.980 --> 02:08:41.817
And we talk about how tragic it is when something happens,

2651
02:08:47.033 --> 02:08:48.957
when somebody dies, somebody gets sick,

2652
02:08:48.957 --> 02:08:50.830
there's an accident, there's a disaster.

2653
02:08:50.830 --> 02:08:55.830
And as though that's out of the norm and in reality,

2654
02:09:07.260 --> 02:09:10.790
the fact that we're alive, it's just a miracle.

2655
02:09:10.790 --> 02:09:13.313
The fact that we're here,

2656
02:09:16.530 --> 02:09:17.760
it becomes really hard to talk about this stuff

2657
02:09:17.760 --> 02:09:19.367
because you can't express it in ways

2658
02:09:19.367 --> 02:09:22.520
that haven't been expressed in just such cliche terms

2659
02:09:22.520 --> 02:09:25.900
but the fact that we're living and breathing

2660
02:09:25.900 --> 02:09:27.683
and talking and all of this,

2661
02:09:29.130 --> 02:09:30.590
with everything that's going on.

2662
02:09:30.590 --> 02:09:32.940
On a space rock, yeah exactly,

2663
02:09:32.940 --> 02:09:35.377
that emits oxygen that we can breathe.

2664
02:09:35.377 --> 02:09:38.730
And we give it back CO2 as we're hurdling through space.

2665
02:09:38.730 --> 02:09:39.563
I mean, it's all insane, right?

2666
02:09:39.563 --> 02:09:41.010
That spinning 16.

2667
02:09:41.010 --> 02:09:46.010
But our brains can't really grok that whole thing.

2668
02:09:46.430 --> 02:09:48.360
Obviously we intellectually understand

2669
02:09:48.360 --> 02:09:50.410
like that life is a miracle,

2670
02:09:50.410 --> 02:09:53.670
but we can't fully embody that or appreciate that

2671
02:09:53.670 --> 02:09:55.150
on a moment to moment basis.

2672
02:09:55.150 --> 02:10:00.150
And in my experience, people who have experienced

2673
02:10:01.520 --> 02:10:04.635
certain types of trauma or life altering

2674
02:10:04.635 --> 02:10:08.950
situations like yourself come out of those experiences

2675
02:10:08.950 --> 02:10:11.480
with obviously this renewed appreciation,

2676
02:10:11.480 --> 02:10:15.149
but there's kind of a timeline, that dissipates over time.

2677
02:10:15.149 --> 02:10:17.200
It's hard to hold onto that, right?

2678
02:10:17.200 --> 02:10:18.880
And so here you are a year later,

2679
02:10:18.880 --> 02:10:22.950
I feel like you really are holding onto that.

2680
02:10:22.950 --> 02:10:25.610
you really are in the moment,

2681
02:10:25.610 --> 02:10:28.030
when we first sat down and you're like,

2682
02:10:28.030 --> 02:10:30.230
it's great man, I'm so happy.

2683
02:10:30.230 --> 02:10:32.430
I could feel it, that's real.

2684
02:10:32.430 --> 02:10:36.380
And I think an incredible gift for all of us

2685
02:10:36.380 --> 02:10:39.777
to hear you express that for you to fully embody that.

2686
02:10:39.777 --> 02:10:44.170
And a powerful reminder of just how precious all of this

2687
02:10:44.170 --> 02:10:47.860
is and how fleeting and how delicate and fragile.

2688
02:10:47.860 --> 02:10:48.693
Yeah.

2689
02:10:49.760 --> 02:10:50.989
I want a piece of that.

2690
02:10:50.989 --> 02:10:55.570
I wanna feel that on a soul level,

2691
02:10:55.570 --> 02:10:57.570
in the way that you feel it.

2692
02:10:57.570 --> 02:11:02.399
The lucky part is that as good as things are

2693
02:11:02.399 --> 02:11:04.720
they'll get bad again for all of us.

2694
02:11:04.720 --> 02:11:05.553
Right.

2695
02:11:07.980 --> 02:11:10.580
I guess the key is to not waste that suffering

2696
02:11:10.580 --> 02:11:15.580
when it comes, see it as a gift, see it as an opportunity

2697
02:11:17.240 --> 02:11:20.547
to open our eyes and to be able to value what it is

2698
02:11:24.754 --> 02:11:26.023
that we have.

2699
02:11:28.116 --> 02:11:30.610
Just a normal day,

2700
02:11:30.610 --> 02:11:33.040
just realizing how incredible that is.

2701
02:11:33.040 --> 02:11:36.595
And the tragedy is that we don't recognize it

2702
02:11:36.595 --> 02:11:41.595
until after the fact, but everything it's so cyclical,

2703
02:11:41.950 --> 02:11:46.830
everything that is good at anytime we're having a good time,

2704
02:11:46.830 --> 02:11:48.906
a comfortable time, life is good.

2705
02:11:48.906 --> 02:11:52.650
We can be 100% guaranteed that it's going to get bad again,

2706
02:11:52.650 --> 02:11:54.781
and as bad as it is, it can be 100% guaranteed

2707
02:11:54.781 --> 02:11:56.050
that it's going to get good again,

2708
02:11:56.050 --> 02:12:01.050
and it's just being able to see all of it all at once

2709
02:12:04.060 --> 02:12:09.060
and recognize what is good and be able to really value it

2710
02:12:14.050 --> 02:12:14.993
as it's happening.

2711
02:12:16.820 --> 02:12:20.092
Because like I was saying, there is no idealistic future

2712
02:12:20.092 --> 02:12:23.240
where it's gonna all be right.

2713
02:12:23.240 --> 02:12:25.870
And we're just going to be happy.

2714
02:12:25.870 --> 02:12:27.870
Happiness isn't something, we're not a victim of it.

2715
02:12:27.870 --> 02:12:29.717
It doesn't just happen to us.

2716
02:12:29.717 --> 02:12:33.905
It's happening all the time and it's our ability to see it

2717
02:12:33.905 --> 02:12:35.010
and recognize it.

2718
02:12:35.010 --> 02:12:40.010
And yeah, it's just incredible, really.

